  "text": "To solve the problem, first, let's rewrite the given equation in terms of a single variable sum. Consider the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{a_k} + \\frac{1}{a_{k+1}} + \\frac{1}{a_{k+2}} = 0\n\\]\n\nwhich for \\(k=1\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{a_1} + \\frac{1}{a_2} + \\frac{1}{a_3} = 0\n\\]\n\nAnalogously for other values of \\(k\\), but it actually holds true for any \\(k\\): we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{a_{(k+n)}} = 0\n\\]\n\nThus the series is constant, and therefore the constant sum:\n\n\\[\nR_k := \n\\frac{1}{a_1} + \\frac{1}{a_2} + \\frac{1}{a_3} + \\dots + \\frac{1}{a_n} + \\frac{1}{a_1} + \\frac{1}{a_2} + \\frac{1}{a_3} = \n2 \\left( \\frac{1}{a_1} + \\frac{1}{a_2} + \\frac{1}{a_3} \\right) = 0\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we emerge to find to the sum of:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^n \\frac{1}{a_k a_{k+1}} = 0\n\\]\n\nGiven the general form of the \\(\\frac{1}{a}\\)'s, it is bounded by \\(O(n)\\). As this is a 'traingle inequality', we conclude that the value of \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]\n\nis obtained.",
